{
  "sourceId": "2022-P1-Q06",
  "source": "<a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://uat-wp.s3.eu-west-2.amazonaws.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/05/07141241/TMUA-2022-paper-1.pdf#page=8\">TMUA 2022 · Paper 1 · Question 6</a> · <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://uat-wp.s3.eu-west-2.amazonaws.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/06/04105226/TMUA-2022-paper-1-worked-answers.pdf#page=8\">Official worked answer</a>",
  "label": "TMUA 2022 · Paper 1 · Question 6",
  "lead": "<img class=\"source-question\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\" aria-label=\"Enlarge question\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"TMUA 2022 · Paper 1 · Question 6 — Evaluate a power integral with logarithmic bounds and recover the logarithm argument.\">",
  "options": [
    "A",
    "B",
    "C",
    "D",
    "E",
    "F",
    "G"
  ],
  "correct": "F",
  "hints": [
    {
      "title": "Integrate the simple function first",
      "body": "<p>The integrand is just <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>. Find its antiderivative and write its values at the two stated bounds.</p>",
      "recap": "Logarithmic endpoints do not change the power-integration rule.",
      "pitfall": "Square the whole endpoint, not only its logarithm argument.",
      "pause": "Write the definite integral as a difference of two squares.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b1-l20",
          "reminder": "An antiderivative of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Relate the endpoints",
      "body": "<p>Write <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>20</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></math>, and use the product law for logarithms. Set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mn>5</mn></mrow></math> so both endpoints can be expressed using one symbol.</p>",
      "recap": "Related logarithmic arguments often make a difference of squares collapse.",
      "pitfall": "A logarithm of a product is a sum, not a product of logarithms.",
      "pause": "Express the upper bound in terms of the lower and simplify the integral algebraically.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b1-l06",
          "reminder": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>log</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>log</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> for positive arguments."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Recover the requested argument",
      "body": "<p>Use the power law to absorb any multiplier of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mn>5</mn></mrow></math>, and rewrite the remaining constant as a base-two logarithm. Then combine the logarithms.</p>",
      "recap": "Equal logarithms with the same valid base have equal arguments.",
      "pitfall": "The question asks for the argument of the logarithm, not the value of the integral.",
      "pause": "Turn your simplified integral into a single logarithm and identify its argument.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b1-l06",
          "reminder": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><msub><mi>log</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>log</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and addition combines positive arguments by multiplication."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "solution": "<p>Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mn>5</mn></mrow></math>. Then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mn>20</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, so the integral is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>.</p><p>Using logarithm laws, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mn>5</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mn>25</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mn>100</mn></mrow></math>. Since this is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi>M</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow></math>. <strong>Answer F.</strong></p>",
